  - isosceles tetrahedron
  - disphenoid
  - dihedral angle
abstract: >-
  Tran replaced the trisecting planes of the Morley construction for a tetrahedron by
  p-secting planes, with one parameter for each pair of opposite edges, and conjectured that
  a regular Morley tetrahedron forces a pair of opposite edges whose four cross edges are
  equal (arXiv:2609.06553, Conjecture 8.6). We show that every isosceles tetrahedron with
  three different edge lengths is a counterexample: its Morley tetrahedron is regular exactly
  when one vertex lies on one of four lines through the centre, and every such point occurs.
  When all three parameters equal 4, only the regular tetrahedron remains.
references:
  - type: article
    title: "The Converse Problem for the Morley Tetrahedron: Counterexamples, Conjectures, and Partial Results"
